{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790888591,"nonce":"jWtr1FWk07SUD2lB4gRJ/6xeGqE=","title":"Every Remedy needs two approvals however harmless, because no operator-written risk rule exists to say which ones need only one","message":"**What is wrong.** ADR 0054 §3: operator-written rules over the command (verb, resource kind, namespace, reversibility) set the baseline; a model may then only raise a Remedy to double approval, seeing only the command, its target and the rules' verdict. `4148256` makes everything double approval because no rules exist.\n\n**Why it matters.** Double approval on `rollout restart` trains approvers to rubber-stamp, which is the failure the second approver exists to prevent.\n\n**Where.** Risk rule config and Settings UI; a raise-only model check behind the `180a525` model port; the approval screen shows which rule set the tier.\n\n**Scope.** medium.\n\n**Done when.** A rule can mark `rollout restart` in `ns=payments` as single approval; `kubectl delete secret …` under a double rule cannot execute on one approval; `sh -c …` stays double whatever the rules say; the risk model can move single → double and never double → single (asserted by test); its input contains no Step or log content (asserted by test).\n\n**Blocked by.** `4148256`\n\n**Mode.** AFK — can be implemented and merged without a human decision. Source: ADR 0052/0053/0054 design session, 2026-10-02.","files":null}]}